            <div id="ContentTab" class="content active">                                                
    <div class="widget widget-ArticleFulltext widget-instance-OUP_Abstract_Article_FullText_Widget"
         data-widget-name="ArticleFulltext"
         data-widget-instance="OUP_Abstract_Article_FullText_Widget">
        <div class="module-widget">
    <div class="widget-items" data-widgetname="ArticleFulltext">





                    <h2 scrollto-destination=572267414 id="572267414" class="abstract-title js-splitscreen-abstract-title" >Abstract</h2>
<section class="abstract" aria-label="Main abstract"><p class="chapter-para">Peer review depends on unpaid expert labour, but scholars do not treat every invitation as an equally legitimate call to serve. Existing research documents reviewer scarcity, reform initiatives, and mixed incentive effects, yet it says less about how reviewers interpret the social meaning of review work itself. Drawing on 30 critical-incident interviews with reviewers of journals indexed in the Science Citation Index or Social Sciences Citation Index (SCI/SSCI) across disciplines and career stages in China, this study develops a sociological account of peer review as conditional academic citizenship. Reviewers were most willing to contribute when editorial interactions were experienced as doing three things: recognizing their jurisdictional expertise, ensuring that their labour could matter, and returning invisible work as acknowledged contribution. Personalized invitations, negotiable timelines, strong desk screening, clear standards, and post-review closure tended to sustain engagement by framing reviewing as collegial service to a field. Generic requests, low-quality manuscripts, silence after submission, and dismissed concerns were instead commonly described as recasting reviewing as extractive labour. Over time, chronic low-grade devaluation appeared to raise reviewers’ acceptance thresholds, while acute breaches such as misattribution or ignored misconduct could trigger journal blacklisting. By showing how unpaid peer review is organized through recognition, consequentiality, and relational closure, the study advances sociological debates on professional labour, evaluation, socialization, and academic citizenship.</p></section>                    <div class="article-metadata-panel clearfix at-ArticleMetadata"></div>
    <!-- /foreach in Model.Sections -->
